      sources: "Sources: Microsoft Learn, List userProcessingResults; access package assignments. Chapter 03, “Day-one readiness,” “The tests.”",
      say: `Readiness counts the hires whose hire date is today, and calls a hire ready when the account exists and is enabled, holds a license, has the onboarding package delivered, and has a completed pre-hire run. It reads the hires from H R and three exports, each with a read-only permission. Run it on an export taken at the start of the day, because it measures state, not timestamps. The chapter adds nine tests, and all fifty-seven of the kit's tests pass with no tenant or network.`,
      detail: `readiness counts the hires whose hire date is the day of --as-of, and calls a hire ready when the account exists and is enabled, holds a license, has the onboarding package delivered and has a completed pre-hire run. It reads the hires from HR and three exports: the users with their hire date and licenses, the pre-hire workflow's user processing results, and the onboarding package's assignments. Run it on an export taken at the start of the day, because it measures state, not timestamps. Reads (task joiner-readiness): list users, with employeeHireDate and assignedLicenses: User.Read.All, with User-LifeCycleInfo.Read.All for the hire date. List userProcessingResults of the pre-hire workflow: LifecycleWorkflows-Reports.Read.All; delegated role Global Reader or Lifecycle Workflows Administrator. List access package assignments, filtered by package: EntitlementManagement.Read.All. The tests: the nine in the Chapter 03 block of tests/test_joiners_and_access.py: the shipped files lint clean, the bad files raise each rule, both argument names, the task limits, the two ceilings, the stripped body, the plan, the refusal and readiness. The kit's 57 tests pass under Python 3.14 with no package, tenant or network.`,
