      sources: "Sources: Chapter 03, “Federal requirements and evidence”; the guide's Gate 3 requirements table; federal framework crosswalk.",
      say: `What does this give the I S S O? The reviewed files and the plan that changed each version, with its manifest hash: the definitions and versions AC-2 enhancement 1 asks for. The lint's clean run, showing a one-time pass of at most twenty-four hours and an execution limit on every scheduled workflow, which supports IA-5. And the readiness summary for each start date. The pass is a bridge to phishing-resistant MFA, so an account still on a pass after the agreed window is a finding, though this code doesn't read it.`,
      detail: `This chapter supports the guide's Gate 3. The Gate 3 requirements table maps it to NIST SP 800-53 AC-2(1) (automated system account management), IA-5 (the initial authenticator is time-limited and reaches an accountable person) and OMB M-22-09's phishing-resistant MFA, which the pass bridges to; the federal framework crosswalk lists each source. What this code gives the ISSO: the reviewed workflow files and the plan that changed each version, with its manifest's SHA-256, as the definitions and versions AC-2(1) asks for; the lint's clean run, showing a one-time pass of at most 24 hours and an execution limit on every scheduled workflow (IA-5 and the guardrails); the readiness summary per start date, the day-one readiness rate Book 03, Chapter 11 measures; the run history the workflows leave, exported past Entra's 30-day view, which this code doesn't keep (the guide's evidence list says where it goes). Accounts still on a pass after the agreed window, when the card should have been bound (Chapter 05), are a finding for the ISSO; this chapter's code doesn't read them.`,
