      sources: "Sources: Chapter 03, “Dangers.”",
      say: `The dangers are mostly drift and scale. A workflow edited in the portal drifts from its file; every plan warns on it. A scope on a department name silently stops after a reorganization. A long or reusable pass outlives the card's arrival. A rehire whose old account kept its methods can't get a pass, so rehires need their own path. With no execution limit, a bad H R load runs to the end. A delete in the leaver removes the account before devices are retired. And a readiness figure from a midday export counts late fixes as ready.`,
      detail: `A workflow edited in the portal: the tenant drifts from the reviewed file; guard: JW-15 in every plan; the file is the only source of a new version. A scope on a department name: a reorganization renames the department and the workflow silently stops; JW-02. A long or reusable pass: the bridge outlives the card's arrival; JW-05. A rehire whose old account kept its methods: the pass task fails for them; Learn's prerequisite stated in the chapter; the rehire workflow needs its own path (guide, Step 3). No execution limit: a bad HR load runs to the end; JW-08; VK-HR-06 reads the limit back. A new hire added to an app group directly: access no review or package knows about; JW-09. Delete in the leaver: the account is gone before devices are retired; JW-11. A plan from broken files: the mistake reaches the tenant as a new version; plan refuses while a Finding stands. More workflows than the licensing page allows: the tenant hits a ceiling Learn states two ways; JW-07 holds 50. A readiness figure from a midday export: late fixes count as ready; run readiness on the start-of-day export.`,
